// Should fail: `Slot.new()` on a generic class, written where the class's type
// parameters are not in scope, does not say what the object holds. Inside one of
// Slot's own methods the same call means `Slot<T>.new()` and is accepted, with T
// bound by the instantiation the method was specialized for.
//
// It has to be rejected rather than built: each instantiation is laid out
// separately (issue #381), so an object with no instantiation to name has fields
// that cannot be read or written. Rejected in semantics, where there is a span
// to point at, rather than in codegen, which would report a user's own program
// as an internal compiler error (issue #360).

class Slot<T> {
    T item
}

func main() returns void {
    auto s = Slot.new()
    return
}
